    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/875/cw/en-us/job/503983/tl-senior-project-analyst": {
      "title": "TL Senior Project Analyst",
      "description": "TL Senior Project Analyst Apply now Job No: 503983 Work Type: Staff Full Time (1500 hours or greater) Location: Dayton, OH Category: Research Institute Staff Department: Sustainment Bus Support Ser - 250723 Pay Grade: P3 - Exempt Advertised: October 21, 2026 Applications close: November 02, 2026 Position Summary: Join UDRI as a TL Senior Project Analyst! This position provides contract, financial, and project analysis support across a broad portfolio of sustainment programs. The role requires a strong understanding of project and program management principles, government contracting processes, and financial tracking. The Project Analyst supports senior program managers, engineers, and technical leads by managing project finances, monitoring deliverables, coordinating contract actions, and generating performance insights. In addition to core analyst duties, this position includes Team Lead responsibilities, supporting workflow coordination, mentoring junior analysts, enhancing process consistency, and assisting leadership in maintaining an effective and collaborative project support environment. The majority of work supports government customers, with some engagement from industry partners. Essential Duties & Responsibilities • Track and manage contract deliverables, coordinate contract actions, and support proposal development. • Review contract terms, monitor compliance, and identify impacts to scope, schedule, or funding. • Maintain and analyze project budgets, burn rates, financial performance, and expenditure tracking. • Execute forecasting, variance analysis, spend planning, and reconcile/validate invoices and cost documentation. • Prepare financial summaries, dashboards, and monthly reporting packages for leadership and customers. • Support PMs and engineers with schedule tracking, resource forecasting, logistics coordination, and deliverable management. • Maintain project data systems, including risk/issue/action item tracking, shared folders, and action logs. • Compile and analyze financial, contractual, and project data to provide decision-support insights. • Coordinate communication across government customers, subcontractors, and internal functional teams. • Identify inefficiencies, recommend process improvements, and contribute to inventory, audit, and cross-team initiatives. Team Lead Responsibilities • Serve as a workflow coordinator for junior project analysts, ensuring consistent processes and high-quality outputs. • Provide mentoring, training, and guidance on UDRI policies, contract workflows, deliverable tracking, and financial tools. • Review analysts’ work for accuracy, consistency, and compliance with internal standards. • Recommend enhancements to tools, templates, and reporting methods to increase efficiency across the project analyst team. • Support Group Lead and senior program managers in identifying training needs, assignment planning, and task prioritization. • Help foster a collaborative, agile environment focused on accuracy, timeliness, and customer satisfaction. • Act as a liaison between analysts and program leadership to ensure clarity in expectations and alignment with sector objectives. Minimum Qualifications: • Bachelor’s degree in business, finance, accounting, project management, engineering technology, or a related field. • 6+ years of directly related experience in project management support, financial analysis, contract administration, or program coordination. • Ability to independently perform the vast majority of responsibilities, exercising judgment in determining methods, approaches, and appropriate techniques. • Strong and in-depth knowledge of project or financial analysis standards, principles, and practices, including wide application of PM, financial, or contract management concepts. • Experience developing solutions for problems requiring integration of multiple factors, creativity, and thoroughness. • Ability to serve as a team member or informal team leader for projects or workflows within a professional work group. • Strong communication skills with ability to represent the organization as a prime contact on contracts or projects, interacting with senior internal/external personnel. • Proficiency with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel, for budget reconciliation, spend plans, deliverable tracking, and financial reporting. • Due to requirements of our research contracts with the U.S. federal government, candidates for this position must be a U.S. Citizen. Preferred Qualifications: While not everyone may possess all of the preferred qualifications, the ideal candidate will bring many of the following: • Master’s degree in business administration, finance, project management, engineering management, or related field. • 8+ years of directly related experience in a government contracting, defense sustainment, or R&D support environment. • Experience providing technical guidance or mentorship to less experienced staff or analysts. • Demonstrated ability to anticipate problems, identify solutions, and support decisions that influence project schedules, client relationships, or expenditures. • Prior experience preparing cost proposals, spend plans, financial review packages, and contract deliverables for government customers. • Familiarity with project management methodologies, reporting standards, and common risk/issue tracking practices. • Experience leading or coordinating cross-functional workflows (finance, contracts, engineering, PM). Special Instructions to Applicants: To apply please submit a cover letter addressing each minimum qualification and any applicable preferred qualifications that you meet. Closing Statement: Informed by its Catholic and Marianist mission, the University is committed to the dignity of every human being. Informed by this commitment, we seek to increase diversity in all of its forms, achieve fair outcomes, and model inclusion across our campus community. The University is committed to policies of affirmative action designed to increase the employment opportunities of individuals with disabilities and protected veterans in compliance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and Vietnam Era Veterans' Readjustment Assistance Act of 1973. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/875/cw/en-us/job/503957/associate-systems-engineer": {
      "title": "Associate Systems Engineer",
      "description": "Associate Systems Engineer Apply now Job No: 503957 Work Type: Staff Full Time (1500 hours or greater) Location: Dayton, OH Category: Research Institute Staff Department: Space Power Technologies - 250662 Pay Grade: R1 - Exempt Advertised: October 19, 2026 Applications close: October 30, 2026 Position Summary: New ideas surround us, but only a few will change the world. We are building upon RPS design philosophies that have stood the test of time, and this is your opportunity to become part of a team that brings us one step closer. If you desire to create something that lasts a lifetime and beyond, you’re ready for UDRI. The University of Dayton Research Institute (UDRI) Space Power Systems Office develops advanced power technologies that enable the next generation of space exploration missions. Working with sponsors including NASA, the Department of Defense, the Department of Energy, and commercial industry, our multidisciplinary teams research, design, prototype, test, and qualify novel space power systems capable of operating in some of the harshest environments ever encountered. We are seeking an early-career Systems Engineer to support the development of advanced radioisotope power systems (RPS), spacecraft power architectures, thermal systems, and related aerospace technologies. Working alongside experienced engineers and scientists, the successful candidate will participate in systems engineering, digital engineering, hardware integration, testing, and technology maturation activities spanning concept development through prototype demonstration. This position is intended to provide progressive experience across systems engineering, design, analysis, assembly, integration, and qualification activities, preparing engineers for increasing technical responsibility as they develop within the organization. It also provides an excellent opportunity to develop technical breadth across multiple engineering disciplines while contributing to pioneering aerospace programs supporting national security and space exploration. Responsibilities: Systems Engineering • Assist in developing system requirements, interface definitions, verification methods, and technical documentation. • Support development and maintenance of system architectures and technical baselines. • Participate in engineering trade studies and technical evaluations. • Apply systems engineering, MBSE, and digital engineering tools under senior engineer mentorship. Engineering Analysis • Support engineering analyses including structural, thermal, electrical, and reliability assessments. • Assist in interpreting analysis results and documenting engineering recommendations. • Support development of verification and validation plans. Assembly, Integration & Test • Support assembly, integration, checkout, and testing of prototype hardware. • Assist in preparing test procedures, data packages, and technical documentation. • Participate in laboratory testing and data collection. • Help investigate technical issues identified during assembly and testing. Customer Support • Participate in technical meetings with government and industry sponsors. • Prepare technical presentations and engineering documentation. • Support proposal preparation and technical reports. Professional Development • Develop technical knowledge in systems engineering, spacecraft power systems, and qualification methodologies through mentoring and project experience. • Continuously improve engineering skills through formal training and hands-on project participation. Minimum Qualifications: • B.S. degree in Systems Engineering, or related engineering discipline. • At least two years of engineering experience. • Fundamental understanding of engineering principles. • Familiarity with CAD software. • Strong analytical and problem-solving skills. • Excellent written and verbal communication. • Ability to work effectively on multidisciplinary engineering teams. • Due to the requirements of our research contracts with the U.S. federal government, candidates for this position must be a U.S. citizen. • Ability to obtain a DoD Secret Clearance. Preferred Qualifications: While not everyone may possess all of the preferred qualifications, the ideal candidate will bring many of the following: • Master's degree in Aerospace, Mechanical, Electrical, Materials, Systems Engineering, or related engineering discipline. • Internship, co-op, undergraduate research, or graduate research experience. • Exposure to systems engineering principles. • Familiarity with CAD software. • Exposure to MATLAB, Python, LabVIEW, or similar engineering software. • Familiarity with model-based systems engineering concepts. • Experience participating in engineering design projects. • Interest in aerospace systems, spacecraft, power systems, or energy technologies. Special Instructions to Applicants: To apply please submit a cover letter addressing each minimum qualification and any applicable preferred qualifications that you meet. Closing Statement: Informed by its Catholic and Marianist mission, the University is committed to the dignity of every human being. Informed by this commitment, we seek to increase diversity in all of its forms, achieve fair outcomes, and model inclusion across our campus community. The University is committed to policies of affirmative action designed to increase the employment opportunities of individuals with disabilities and protected veterans in compliance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and Vietnam Era Veterans' Readjustment Assistance Act of 1973. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/875/cw/en-us/job/503967/assistant-professor-coordinator-of-cataloging-and-metadata": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor & Coordinator of Cataloging and Metadata",
      "description": "Assistant Professor & Coordinator of Cataloging and Metadata Apply now Job No: 503967 Work Type: Faculty Full Time Location: Dayton, OH Category: Faculty Department: Collection Strategies and Services - 240100 Advertised: October 19, 2026 Applications close: November 09, 2026 Position Summary: Do you enjoy working in collaborative library environments? Are you interested in a tenure track faculty position at a university that’s small enough to really get to know people and make connections, but large enough to have the resources you need to succeed? The University of Dayton Libraries are looking for a Coordinator of Cataloging and Metadata to lead and support our cataloging and metadata work, foster collaboration, and help shape the future of library discovery and access. Apply today! This is a 12-month, full-time, 35 hours per week, tenure track faculty position at the rank of assistant professor with an anticipated start date of February 1, 2027. The University of Dayton offers a strong benefits package, including tuition remission for self and immediate family, 22 vacation days, and 17 University holidays, including the week between Christmas and New Year’s Day. Reporting to the Director of Collections Strategies and Services, the Coordinator of Cataloging and Metadata coordinates and manages the development, implementation, and evaluation of metadata (MARC and non-MARC) workflows that support the discovery and curation of the Libraries collections, including both print and digital content, across all divisions of the University Libraries. About the University Libraries Collections Strategies and Services Division The Collections Strategies and Services division manages the lifecycle, budget, organization, and accessibility of the university’s digital and print collections, including acquisitions, electronic resource management, cataloging and metadata, discovery tools, and collection assessment. Faculty and staff in the division utilize library platforms such as Alma and Primo to support resource management, discovery, and access. Responsibilities: Coordinates cataloging and metadata operations across the University Libraries, including creation, transformation, remediation, maintenance, enhanced cataloging, and original cataloging for diverse collections and formats, including rare books. Oversees cataloging and authority control activities to ensure compliance with local, consortial, and national standards, including RDA, LC-PCC Policy Statements, and Program for Cooperative Cataloging (PCC) guidelines, while contributing to the national authority file through NACO. Supervises, trains, and provides guidance to library staff performing cataloging/metadata activities in the division (currently 2 FTE). Applied fluency with existing and emerging metadata standards, tools, best practices, and AI technologies, as well as developments in interoperability, with a focus on standards such as Dublin Core, BIBFRAME, and MARCXML and the evolving role of AI in metadata creation, enrichment, and management. Coordinates the development, implementation, and maintenance of metadata strategies, profiles, standards, and good practices for describing the Libraries' distinctive digital content. Provides metadata expertise for projects involving digital collections, system migrations, metadata remediation, and selection of appropriate metadata crosswalks between systems and standards. Serves as a collections liaison to assigned departments and serves on the liaison team. Manages the Roesch Library book donation workflow and maintains accurate donation records. Collaborates with the state-wide consortium to focus on cataloging and metadata as it relates to integration between shared systems and local systems, authority work, database integrity and impact of displays and searching of metadata on end users in shared or integrated systems by serving on the OhioLINK Database Improvement and Discoverability Policy Team. Serves on current and emerging cross-divisional teams within the Libraries, such as the Digital Stewardship and Sustainability Team and Collections and Scholarly Communications Team as appropriate. Contributes to the implementation of the Libraries’ and University’s strategic priorities, including inclusive excellence. Conducts research and disseminates the findings through publication in peer-reviewed journals, major national meetings, and other venues. Serves on various library, University, and professional committees. University Libraries Consisting of the Roesch Library, the internationally recognized Marian Library, and University Archives and Special Collections, the collective University Libraries are home to over 2 million print and digital materials. The University of Dayton is a proud charter member institution of OhioLINK, a widely acknowledged innovative leader among library consortia that collaboratively shares resources and services in medical, music, art, and academic library institutions throughout the state of Ohio. The Libraries at UD serve as a central hub of campus intellectual and cultural life and underwent a successful renovation directly informed by campus and community needs. Supporting over 400,000 visits annually, the dedicated Libraries faculty and staff are deeply engaged in and committed to furthering the mission of empowering learning, scholarship and creative expression. The University Grounded in its Catholic, Marianist tradition , The University of Dayton is a comprehensive university offering 70 majors to undergraduates, and 50 programs (12 doctoral) to graduate students. UD provides education to develop the whole student and is committed to social justice and experiential learning. UD enrolls approximately 9,000 students, including 6,900 full-time undergraduates. It is one of the nation’s largest Catholic universities and one of the largest private universities in Ohio. At the University of Dayton, we value inclusive excellence because we recognize that diversity, equity and inclusion are fundamental to academic and institutional excellence. Inclusive excellence requires a comprehensive, cohesive and collaborative alignment of infrastructure, resources and actions. We strive to be active, intentional, and sustain engagement with and celebration of diversity in every dimension of institutional life. Because we seek a workforce with a wide range of perspectives and experiences, we encourage all candidates to apply. The City of Dayton Located in Dayton, Ohio, our university offers more than just a rewarding career. With a burgeoning downtown area, Dayton boasts extensive green space and over 350 miles of biking/hiking trails , with ample opportunities for outdoor recreation and relaxation. With a low cost of living, you can enjoy a comfortable lifestyle while pursuing your professional interests. Additionally, Dayton's dynamic arts scene offers a rich cultural experience. It's a great place to live, work, and thrive. Go to Destination Dayton Dayton for more information. Minimum Qualifications: MLS, MLIS, or MSIS from an ALA-accredited institution. At least one year of successful professional experience in cataloging, including: Cataloging rare books or special collections materials, including original and complex copy cataloging. Knowledge of cataloging and classification practices, standards, and schema such as MARC, RDA, LCSH, and PCC standards (BIBCO, NACO). Knowledge of Ex Libris Alma and OCLC Connexion. Experience with one or more of the following XML-based standards: Dublin Core, EAD, MARCXML, OAI-PMH, VRA Core, METS, and PREMIS. Excellent written communication skills. Articulated ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment with a strong service orientation. Articulated ability to support the University of Dayton’s commitment to the principles of inclusive excellence. Potential for excellence in librarianship, research, and service. Preferred Qualifications: While not everyone may meet all preferred qualifications, the ideal candidate will bring many of the following, and we are committed to helping our future colleague develop these preferred skills: Demonstrated ability to plan, initiate, and implement projects, workflows, and services. Supervisory or team leadership experience. Familiarity with linked data principles and BIBFRAME. Knowledge of book and book publishing history. Experience supporting collection development and acquisitions, including evaluating and selecting resources and responding to faculty requests for print and electronic books. Awareness of critical concerns in the field, including bias in resource description and access practices, as well as the ethical implications and potential biases of AI in metadata and cataloging practices. Experience fostering inclusive excellence in the workplace, including working with and supporting individuals from culturally and socially diverse communities. Effective interpersonal communication skills. Effective oral communication skills. Commitment to breadth of education including educating the whole person in the Marianist tradition and a commitment to social justice and service to the community, university and profession. Special Instructions to Applicants: To apply please submit: Cover letter describing how minimum and preferred qualifications are met. CV. Statement addressing inclusive excellence and how that informs your work. Contact information for 3 professional references who may be contacted later in the process, Unofficial transcripts. Please scan into one document for upload. Candidates invited to a campus interview will be expected to deliver a presentation about a topic specified 2 weeks in advance. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis. The University does not provide work visa sponsorship for this position. Posting closes at 11:55 PM EST November 9, 2026. Closing Statement: Informed by its Catholic and Marianist mission, the University is committed to the dignity of every human being. Informed by this commitment, we seek to increase diversity in all of its forms, achieve fair outcomes, and model inclusion across our campus community. The University is committed to policies of affirmative action designed to increase the employment opportunities of individuals with disabilities and protected veterans in compliance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1973. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/875/cw/en-us/job/503969/assistant-professor-business-librarian": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor & Business Librarian",
      "description": "Assistant Professor & Business Librarian Apply now Job No: 503969 Work Type: Faculty Full Time Location: Dayton, OH Category: Faculty Department: Teaching, Research & Engagement - 240110 Advertised: October 19, 2026 Applications close: November 09, 2026 Position Summary: Do you enjoy working in collaborative library environments? Are you interested in a tenure track faculty position at a university that’s small enough to really get to know people and make connections, but large enough to have the resources you need to succeed? The University of Dayton Libraries are looking for a Business Librarian to join our team of colleagues. Apply today! This is a 12-month, full-time, 35 hours per week, tenure track faculty position at the rank of assistant professor with an anticipated start date of January 1, 2027. The University of Dayton offers a strong benefits package, including tuition remission for self and immediate family, 22 vacation days, and 17 University holidays, including the week between Christmas and New Year’s Day. Reporting to the Director of Teaching, Research, and Engagement, the Business Librarian provides research support, curriculum-integrated instruction, and collection development for the School of Business Administration and its associated centers, while advancing information literacy across the curriculum and supporting strategic library initiatives. Responsibilities: Serve as a liaison to the School of Business Administration (SBA) including research support, instruction, and collection development. Design, conduct, and assess curriculum-integrated, scaffolded instruction in the liaison areas both in person and online, in consultation with SBA faculty and staff, for undergraduate and graduate business students. Provide in-depth research consultations regarding specialized business resources, including investment and financial data, company and industry information, market research, and economic indicators. Provide research consultation for the L. William Crotty Center for Entrepreneurial Leadership, including collaborations as appropriate with the Hub, the Entrepreneurs’ Center, and/or the Greater West Dayton Incubator. Maintain applied fluency with recent trends in research and teaching related to the liaison areas, including applications of AI and other emerging technologies. Serve on the Instruction Team, including supporting the development and integration of information literacy through Common Academic Program (CAP) general education courses (e.g., HUM 102 seminar and second-year writing program). Oversee library collections for the Business and Social Sciences, respond to faculty and student requests for library materials, and collaborate with other library departments on collections-related issues. Serves on the Collections and Scholarly Communications team. Contribute to the implementation of the Libraries’ and University’s strategic initiatives, including for inclusive excellence. Conduct research and disseminate the findings through publication in peer-reviewed journals, major national meetings, and other venues. Serve on various library, university, and professional committees. Manage or assist with special projects as assigned. About the University Libraries Teaching, Research, and Engagement Division The Teaching, Research, and Engagement division designs, implements, and assesses information literacy instruction and research support. Faculty and staff in the division collaborate with teams across the Libraries and units on campus and advocate for the needs of library users, with a focus on the Libraries' contributions to student success and belonging. University Libraries Consisting of the Roesch Library, the internationally recognized Marian Library, and University Archives and Special Collections, the collective UD Libraries are home to over 2 million print and digital materials. The University of Dayton is a proud charter member institution of OhioLINK, a widely acknowledged innovative leader among library consortia that collaboratively shares resources and services in medical, music, art, and academic library institutions throughout the state of Ohio. The Libraries at UD serve as a central hub of campus intellectual and cultural life and underwent a successful renovation directly informed by campus and community needs. Supporting over 400,000 visits annually, the dedicated Libraries faculty and staff are deeply engaged in and committed to furthering the mission of empowering learning, scholarship and creative expression. The University Grounded in its Catholic, Marianist tradition , The University of Dayton is a comprehensive university offering 70 majors to undergraduates, and 50 programs (12 doctoral) to graduate students. UD provides education to develop the whole student and is committed to social justice and experiential learning. UD enrolls approximately 9,000 students, including 6,900 full-time undergraduates. It is one of the nation’s largest Catholic universities and one of the largest private universities in Ohio. The City of Dayton Located in Dayton, Ohio, our university offers more than just a rewarding career. With a burgeoning downtown area, Dayton boasts extensive green space and over 350 miles of biking/hiking trails, with ample opportunities for outdoor recreation and relaxation. With a low cost of living, you can enjoy a comfortable lifestyle while pursuing your professional interests. Additionally, Dayton's dynamic arts scene offers a rich cultural experience. It's a great place to live, work, and thrive. Go to Destination Dayton Destination Dayton for more information. At the University of Dayton, we value our inclusive climate because we know that diversity in experiences and perspectives is vital to advancing innovation, critical thinking, solving complex problems, and creating an inclusive academic community. We translate these values into action by seeking individuals who have experience and expertise working with diverse students, colleagues and constituencies. Minimum Qualifications: MLS, MLIS, or MSIS from an ALA-accredited institution. Experience with instruction and reference in an academic, research, or special library. Experience or education working with business disciplines including accounting, economics, marketing, finance, management and decisions sciences, and related information sources and tools. Excellent written communication skills. Articulated ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment with a strong service orientation. Articulated ability to support the University of Dayton’s commitment to the principles of inclusive excellence. Potential for excellence in librarianship, research, and service. Preferred Qualifications: While not everyone may meet all preferred qualifications, the ideal candidate will bring many of the following, and we are committed to helping our future colleague develop these preferred skills: Demonstrated ability to plan, initiate, and implement projects, workflows, and services. Experience supporting collection development and acquisitions, including evaluating and selecting resources and responding to faculty requests for print and electronic books. Experience fostering inclusive excellence in the workplace, including working with and supporting individuals from culturally and socially diverse communities. Effective interpersonal communication skills. Effective oral communication skills. Effective classroom management skills. Commitment to breadth of education including educating the whole person in the Marianist tradition and a commitment to social justice and service to the community, university and profession. Special Instructions to Applicants: To apply, please submit: Cover letter describing how minimum and preferred qualifications are met, CV, Statement addressing inclusive excellence and how that informs your work, Unofficial transcripts. Please scan into one document for upload. Contact information for 3 professional references who will be contacted later in the process. Candidates invited to a campus interview will be expected to deliver a presentation about a topic specified in advance. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis. The University does not provide work visa sponsorship for this position. Posting closes at 11:55 PM EST on November 9, 2026. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/875/cw/en-us/job/504010/assistant-director-financial-aid-service": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Financial Aid Service",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Financial Aid Service Apply now Job No: 504010 Work Type: Staff Full Time (1500 hours or greater) Location: Dayton, OH Category: University Staff Department: Student Enrollment Services - 202100 Pay Grade: A - Exempt Advertised: October 30, 2026 Applications close: Position Summary: Make a Difference in Every Student’s Journey Through Financial Empowerment and Support. As a Catholic, Marianist research University, we are a diverse community committed to educating the whole person and linking learning and scholarship with leadership and service. Enrollment Management is focused on service excellence to recruit, enroll and graduate highly qualified students and improve access, affordability, inclusion, and success. Reporting to the Senior Associate Director, Flyer Student Services and Financial Aid Service, this staff member is responsible for providing financial aid service to students at the University of Dayton. This individual will serve as part of the counseling team supporting escalated financial aid conversations referred by the Flyer Student Services staff and EdAmerica. As a member of the Flyer Student Services team, this position supports students in decisions about financing their educational expenses and ultimately their academic success, as well as providing support to Flyer Student Services counselors as they counsel students and student workers. Responsibilities include educating students and families about the financial aid process, reviewing FAFSA applications, determining and adjusting all types of financial assistance, providing education regarding loans and various financing options as well as exercising professional judgment. This individual is expected to use problem-solving skills on a daily basis and to routinely exercise a high level of independent professional judgment. The Assistant Director will support the administration of a state financial aid program and serve as the lead program manager to ensure information is awarded accurately and shared with staff and students effectively. The Assistant Director is also focused on driving service improvements by advancing key internal partnerships with the Offices of Student Accounts, the Registrar, Holistic Advisors and other campus partners. This staff member will collaboratively advance these partnerships and use them to drive efficiencies and broaden our service outreach. Further, this individual proactively identifies, recommends, and implements changes in processes to improve services and reduce manual steps for staff administering financial aid. The Assistant Director will support Financial Literacy efforts for undergraduate students across campus. As a Catholic, Marianist University, we believe that inclusive excellence is essential to the success of students and staff. The Assistant Director will work individually and as a member of a team to make tangible contributions to UD’s mission. All employees are empowered to attend workshops, seminars, and other educational experiences to deepen their understanding of our mission and strategic priorities. Minimum Qualifications: * Bachelor’s Degree OR three years of experience working in financial aid with progressive growth in responsibilities. * At least 2 years of experience providing service in a professional, fast-paced, deadline-driven environment. * The ability to work with multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. * Strong written and oral communication skills and attention to detail. * Problem solving skills. * Ability to successfully improve and streamline processes. * The successful candidate must be self-motivated and have the ability to work independently. * Ability to provide leadership as a collaborative team member. Preferred Qualifications: While not everyone may possess all of the preferred qualifications, the ideal candidate will bring many of the following: * 2 years of financial aid or campus one stop office experience in a professional, fast-paced, deadline-driven environment with a primary focus on communication, counseling skills, and the ability to solve complex problems. * Master’s Degree in Business, Higher Education Administration, or a related field. * Enrollment Management or Higher Education experience at a private University. * Knowledge and experience with Ellucian Banner software and Salesforce CRM. * Demonstrated effective interpersonal skills. * Demonstrated knowledge of student Financial Aid laws and regulations. * Ability to work with staff, students, and families in a manner consistent with Catholic and Marianist values and principles. Special Instructions to Applicants: To apply please submit a cover letter addressing each minimum qualification and any applicable preferred qualifications that you meet. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis. The University does not provide work visa sponsorship for this position. Closing Statement: Informed by its Catholic and Marianist mission, the University is committed to the dignity of every human being. Informed by this commitment, we seek to increase diversity in all of its forms, achieve fair outcomes, and model inclusion across our campus community. The University is committed to policies of affirmative action designed to increase the employment opportunities of individuals with disabilities and protected veterans in compliance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and Vietnam Era Veterans' Readjustment Assistance Act of 1973. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    }
